  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1127 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nTonight and Wednesday:\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening shows our recent weather maker in \nthe form of a seasonably strong upper-level trough continuing to \npush to the northeast across the mid-Mississippi Valley towards the \nGreat Lakes, while more compact trough pushes across the Great \nBasin. Locally we're dealing with the leftovers from the larger \nsystem to the east, with lingering low stratus, gusty winds and \nareas of light rain and drizzle making for a dreary evening for \nmany. Cooler air wrapping around with the gusty winds will take our \ncurrent temperatures down a few more degrees, bottoming out in the \nupper 30s to low 40s with lighter winds greeting folks heading out \nthe door early. On the edge of the clouds near the north-central NE \nand SD border, fog has developed, leaking slightly eastward. Some of \nthose lower visibilities (one mile or less) may try and reach into \nwestern Knox/Antelope/Boone Counties between 5-8 AM, with those \nchances being around 20%. \n\nHeading through Wednesday, we'll kick most of the precipitation\nfrom the overnight and early morning hours, in favor of a crisp\nfall day that tops out in the upper 40s to mid 50s. With the \nsurface pressure gradient lessening through the day, winds will \nfollow suit before dropping gusts later in the afternoon. Clouds\nwill still be hard to kick, as the back end of the deformation \naxis with the previous system joins with PVA from the incoming \nshortwave to the west. CAMs indicate that some very light \nprecipitation is possible as the best forcing moves through, but\nwe'll be on the dry side of things when it comes to moisture in\nthe column. Those chances build into northeast Nebraska after 6\nPM, arriving to the I-80 corridor around midnight before \nexiting the area by 7 AM Thursday.\n\nThursday and Beyond:\n\nWe continue to struggle to kick the clouds Thursday and Friday, with \nsimilar highs in the upper 40s to low 50s on tap to close out the \nwork week. Forecast soundings show a shallow boundary layer \nThursday, with steep enough lapse rates for some areas to see some \nsprinkles despite how shallow it is. Breaks in the clouds become \nlarger Friday despite a positively-tilted shortwave moving \nsoutheastward through the area -- further evidence that moisture \nreturn into the area continues to be nonexistent. \n\nClouds fully break this weekend and temperatures change in tune, \nstepping up into the 50s Saturday before rising to the 60s Sunday to \nmake for a wonderful weekend to be outside. The ridge to the west of \nthe area nudges eastward just enough to ward off a weak clipper \nshortwave before deamplifying into quasi-zonal flow headed through \nthe upcoming work week.",
